A||||6||87PegasusGT||Racing Career. How?||||1||
Z||000000||87PegasusGT||02-25-2000||04:07 PM||||Hey guys, <BR>I enjoy racing more than any one single activity....except maybe 1...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Anyhow, any suggestions to get started on a career as such?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||02-25-2000||05:33 PM||||All depends on what kind of racing you want to do and how much $$ you have.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||02-25-2000||06:37 PM||||Assuming you are interested in road-racing, call up the regional chapter of the SCCA, (Sports Car Club of America) and ask them for some info. They should be able to guide you in the right direction. Ask for a copy of the GCR (General Competition Rules? I think that's what it's called). It will give you all the info about what cars fit in which classes, allowed modifications, etc. <BR>BTW... A lot of racers actually start out as track workers (corner workers, timing & scoring, etc.) It's a good way to learn the sport, and get familiar with what goes on behind the scenes. <BR>If drag racing is your thing, I can't offer any suggestions, but I'm sure there are some folks here that can.  <p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 02-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||SpeedPhreak||02-25-2000||06:38 PM||||cars- start w/go karts.  & im only 1/2 joking.   its cheap & available & u will meet people that have more info.  also the scca (?)... the autocross organization.  do a search here & im sure there will be info on it.<P>i want to road race bikes... if thats what u want (the cheapest form of pro racing i know of)...  get a rs125 & ask around at a bike shop when/where the club races are.  then move move up in your club from novice to pro & race more bikes (250's 600's open class).  after 2 or 3 seasons u can get an ama license.  then if u have the entry fee & qualify u can race any of the races or the whole pro series... right next to the factory teams & sponsored riders.||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||02-25-2000||08:50 PM||||Drag racing is pretty easy to get involved in. Most strips have one night set aside for the general public. It's called grudge night around here. You pay seven dollars (insert the proper local equivalent amount here) and get to run down the track a few times against other average Joe Blows. You have some safety rules, like wearing a helmet, etc. You get to practice. You basically make it or break it with your starts off the line. It's a rush! Running side by side against another car that's running flat out and screaming, same as you.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierospeeder||02-25-2000||10:41 PM||||Im also interested in doing car races, but probably not as a career, MAYBE.  Next jeff gordan in a 500hp fiero.  <BR>I thought about going to those car schools, its about 2000 bucks for a weekend.  I seen a dragracing school where you can drive a dragster.  There was another school which i like, its in arizona, you get to drive corvettes and learn formula 1 type racing.<P>I know jeff gordan started out with go-karts when he was at a very young age, and i wished i grew up like that.  Not all of us live in the country.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Pretender||02-26-2000||02:45 AM||||There are a few very simple rules to be the next "jeff gordan"<P>1. your daddy must of been into the same catagory/raceing preferance<P>2. Your family must be better then well of, by that i mean at least a summer/winter/spring/fall homes all worth over 250k.<P>3. Gain weight! the average stockcar driver weighs in at @ 190lbs!(seen that on ESPN)<P>4. Your wife must have a modeling career.<P>5. And the most important rule of all, you must have a lower iq then your brother in-law...hahaha!..||||reg||10
